Professor Thompson: Good morning, everyone. Today we''re going to discuss one of the most fascinating topics in astrophysics: black holes. A black hole is a region in space where the gravitational pull is so strong that nothing, including light, can escape. It''s formed when a massive star collapses in on itself and its gravity becomes so strong that it warps the fabric of spacetime around it. The event horizon is the point of no return, where the gravitational pull is so strong that anything that crosses it will be trapped forever. Black holes come in different sizes, ranging from small, stellar-mass black holes formed from the collapse of individual stars, to supermassive black holes found at the centers of galaxies. These supermassive black holes can have masses millions or even billions of times that of our sun. The study of black holes has revolutionized our understanding of the universe, and has led to many breakthroughs in our understanding of gravity and the behavior of matter in extreme environments. Thank you for joining me today.

