Professor Thompson: Good morning, everyone, and welcome to today''s webinar on linguistic theories. As we all know, language is a complex and multifaceted phenomenon that has been studied by scholars for centuries. In this presentation, we will be exploring some of the key theories that have shaped our understanding of language, including structuralism, functionalism, and post-structuralism. We will also be discussing the implications of these theories for language teaching and learning. So, let''s get started. First, let''s take a look at structuralism. This theory, developed by Ferdinand de Saussure, posits that language is a system of signs that are used to convey meaning. According to structuralism, the meaning of a word or phrase is determined by its relationship to other words and phrases in the language. For example, the word ''dog'' has a different meaning in English than it does in French, because the words that surround it in the language are different. Next, let''s consider functionalism. This theory, developed by J.R. Firth, argues that language is a tool that is used to achieve social and communicative goals. According to functionalism, the meaning of a word or phrase is determined by its function in a particular context. For example, the word ''hello'' is used to greet someone, but it can also be used to bid farewell. Finally, let''s look at post-structuralism. This theory, developed by Jacques Derrida, argues that language is a complex and dynamic system that is constantly changing. According to post-structuralism, meaning is not fixed, but is instead determined by the context in which it is used. For example, the word ''bank'' can refer to a financial institution, but it can also refer to the side of a river. In conclusion, these three theories have shaped our understanding of language and its applications. They have also had a significant impact on language teaching and learning. Thank you for joining me today, and I hope you have found this presentation informative and thought-provoking.

